 The RIDER: his state and how he decides to move, one frame at a time.

 A port of wasm/rider.zig.

 THREE THINGS HAPPEN EACH FRAME. He picks a lean by BINARY SEARCH -- the held lean
 whose projected arc stays on the road and ends nearest an asymptotic centre
 target. He picks a forward acceleration, taking the most restrictive of several
 brakes. Then the physics integrates both, and if his real position has crossed
 into the next segment he is re-expressed in that segment's frame.

 THE BINARY SEARCH IS THE HAZARD NOTES WARNS ABOUT. Twelve iterations, each
 deciding on a float comparison after simulating up to two thousand physics steps.
 A difference of 1e-7 between f32 and f64 will eventually flip one of those
 comparisons, and from there the two rides are on different trajectories -- so a
 long trace comparison WILL go red and it will NOT mean the port is wrong. The
 check therefore compares ONE STEP FROM A SHARED STATE and never a trajectory.

 Deferred exactly as the zig defers them, and for the same reason: the cat's
 drawing and pose machinery is out of scope, but `cat-in-danger` is arithmetic and
 is ported, so the throttle hold is real here rather than a no-op.

 We say:

Section: Physics Constants

  yaw-per-tilt : Real
  yaw-per-tilt = 0.1

 The route never banks past this, and the focal pull-in normalises the lean by it.

  max-lean : Real
  max-lean = 20.0 * deg

  a-accel : Real
  a-accel = 0.010

  v-max : Real
  v-max = 2.5

  approach-intersection-dist : Real
  approach-intersection-dist = 60.0

  straighten-margin : Real
  straighten-margin = 0.05

  turn-danger-steps : Integer
  turn-danger-steps = 2000

  min-forward-progress : Real
  min-forward-progress = 25.0

  tilt-hold : Real
  tilt-hold = 2.0 * deg

  brake-decay : Real
  brake-decay = 20.0

  asymptote-tuning : Real
  asymptote-tuning = 0.30

  center-lane-epsilon : Real
  center-lane-epsilon = 0.04

  max-tilt-correction : Real
  max-tilt-correction = 1.0 * deg

  lean-search-iters : Integer
  lean-search-iters = 12

Section: One Physics Step

 A lean tip and an acceleration, integrated. He leans FIRST, then that tilt yaws
 the heading, then the new speed along the MID heading moves the position -- which
 is a midpoint rule, not an Euler step, and the halving is load-bearing.

 The absolute heading turns by the same delta as the frame-relative yaw.

  simulate-rider-step : RiderState, Real, Real -> RiderState
  simulate-rider-step (s) (tilt-step) (accel) =
    let tilt = s.tilt + tilt-step
    in let v = s.v + accel
    in let heading-change = yaw-per-tilt * tilt
    in let mid = s.yaw + heading-change / 2.0
    in RiderState { segment = s.segment, along = s.along + v * r-cos mid, across = s.across + v * r-sin mid, yaw = s.yaw + heading-change, v = v, tilt = tilt, heading = s.heading + heading-change, gaze-yaw = s.gaze-yaw, focus = s.focus }

Section: Projecting the Arc

 Project the held-lean, constant-speed arc forward and score how it plays out: off
 a shoulder is a bad lean, net-backward is a disaster, and clearing the forward
 progress is a good lean -- all pinned to the SAME forward, so survivors tie and
 the search then separates them on where they end up across the road.

 Side is which shoulder he left, if any.

  Side =
   | SideLeft
   | SideNone
   | SideRight

  PathSim = record {
    side : Side,
    forward : Real,
    crossed : Boolean,
    end-across : Real,
    frames : Real
  }

 The zig uses 1e9 as the frames answer for the arms that did not leave the road,
 and the shoulder brake only reads frames when side is not none, so the value is a
 placeholder rather than a number. It is kept because it is what the zig stores.

  no-frames : Real
  no-frames = 1000000000.0

  sim-loop : RiderState, Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Integer, RiderState -> PathSim
  sim-loop (start) (left-bound) (right-bound) (start-side) (start-along) (crossed) (i) (phys) =
    if i >= turn-danger-steps then PathSim { side = SideNone, forward = phys.along - start-along, crossed = crossed, end-across = phys.across, frames = no-frames }
    else sim-step start left-bound right-bound start-side start-along crossed i (simulate-rider-step phys 0.0 0.0)

  sim-step : RiderState, Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Integer, RiderState -> PathSim
  sim-step (start) (left-bound) (right-bound) (start-side) (start-along) (crossed0) (i) (phys) =
    let across = phys.across
    in let forward = phys.along - start-along
    in let crossed = if across * start-side < 0.0 then True else crossed0
    in if across < left-bound then PathSim { side = SideLeft, forward = real-min forward min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = real-from-int i }
    else if across > right-bound then PathSim { side = SideRight, forward = real-min forward min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = real-from-int i }
    else if forward < 0.0 then PathSim { side = SideNone, forward = forward, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = no-frames }
    else if forward >= min-forward-progress then PathSim { side = SideNone, forward = min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = no-frames }
    else sim-loop start left-bound right-bound start-side start-along crossed (i + 1) phys

 The bounds widen to wherever he already is, so a rider who starts outside the
 inset is not instantly judged to have left the road.

  simulate-rider-path : RiderState, Segment -> PathSim
  simulate-rider-path (state) (seg) =
    let inset-hw = seg.width / 2.0 - straighten-margin
    in let right-bound = real-max inset-hw state.across
    in let left-bound = real-min (0.0 - inset-hw) state.across
    in sim-loop state left-bound right-bound (r-sign state.across) state.along False 0 state

Section: The Lean Search

  want-more-right : PathSim, Real -> Boolean
  want-more-right (sim) (target) =
   when sim.side
    is SideLeft -> True
    is SideRight -> False
    is SideNone -> sim.end-across < target

 Binary-search the held lean whose arc stays on the road and ends closest to the
 asymptotic centre target. TWELVE ITERATIONS, each running a fresh path
 simulation, and each deciding on one float comparison -- which is exactly why the
 judge never compares a trajectory.

 The target is an asymptote rather than the centre line: he aims at a fraction of
 his current offset, so he converges on the middle without ever committing to a
 lean that would overshoot it. Dead centre gets a signed epsilon instead, because
 a target of exactly zero has no side to lean toward.

  lean-target : Real -> Real
  lean-target (across) =
    if real-abs across < center-lane-epsilon then (if across >= 0.0 then center-lane-epsilon else 0.0 - center-lane-epsilon)
    else across * asymptote-tuning

  with-tilt : RiderState, Real -> RiderState
  with-tilt (s) (t) =
    RiderState { segment = s.segment, along = s.along, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw, v = s.v, tilt = t, heading = s.heading, gaze-yaw = s.gaze-yaw, focus = s.focus }

  search-lean : RiderState, Segment, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  search-lean (state) (seg) (target) (lo) (hi) (i) =
    if i >= lean-search-iters then (lo + hi) / 2.0
    else search-step state seg target lo hi i ((lo + hi) / 2.0)

  search-step : RiderState, Segment, Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real -> Real
  search-step (state) (seg) (target) (lo) (hi) (i) (mid) =
    if want-more-right (simulate-rider-path (with-tilt state mid) seg) target
      then search-lean state seg target mid hi (i + 1)
      else search-lean state seg target lo mid (i + 1)

  best-tilt-correction : RiderState, Segment -> Real
  best-tilt-correction (state) (seg) =
    search-lean state seg (lean-target state.across) (state.tilt - max-tilt-correction) (state.tilt + max-tilt-correction) 0

Section: Throttle and Brakes

 The safe entry speed for a turn angle: the table from the TypeScript, and every
 angle on this route is tabulated. The comparison is on the ROUNDED degrees, which
 is what makes an exact match on a Real safe here.

  turn-speed : Real -> Real
  turn-speed (angle-rad) =
    let d = round-real (angle-rad * 180.0 / pi)
    in if d ~0 15.0 then 1.297
    else if d ~0 20.0 then 0.840
    else if d ~0 30.0 then 0.461
    else if d ~0 50.0 then 0.222
    else if d ~0 70.0 then 0.139
    else if d ~0 80.0 then 0.117
    else 0.222

 Accelerate, but only while near-upright, and then take the MOST RESTRICTIVE of
 the brakes: reach the corner's entry speed by the commit point, hold the throttle
 for a crossing cat, ease down for the pigs, and do not run off the shoulder.

 The order is the zig's and matters: the cat gate sits after the corner brake and
 before the shoulder brake.

  corner-brake : RiderState, Segment, Real, Real -> Real
  corner-brake (state) (seg) (v-end) (a) =
    let d = seg.commit-along - state.along
    in let corner-a = if d <= 0.000001 then 0.0 else (v-end * v-end - state.v * state.v) / (2.0 * d)
    in if corner-a < a then corner-a else a

 While the cat is in its danger window he stops CLOSING on it -- the acceleration
 goes to zero -- but he never brakes. Coasting in slows his approach, so he reaches
 the final segment later and there is more time for the sunset.

  cat-gate : RiderState, Segment, Real -> Real
  cat-gate (state) (seg) (a) =
    if seg.has-cat then (if a > 0.0 then (if cat-in-danger (seg.cat.along - state.along) state.v then 0.0 else a) else a) else a

  pig-gate : RiderState, Segment, Real -> Real
  pig-gate (state) (seg) (a) =
    let b = pig-gaze-brake state seg
    in if b.engaged then (if b.accel < a then b.accel else a) else a

 The decayed kinematic stop: the further ahead the shoulder is, the gentler the
 brake, and the decay makes a distant shoulder almost free.

  shoulder-brake : RiderState, Segment, Real -> Real
  shoulder-brake (state) (seg) (a) =
    let sim = simulate-rider-path state seg
    in if side-none sim then a else shoulder-brake-at state sim a

  side-none : PathSim -> Boolean
  side-none (sim) =
   when sim.side
    is SideNone -> True
    is SideLeft -> False
    is SideRight -> False

  shoulder-brake-at : RiderState, PathSim, Real -> Real
  shoulder-brake-at (state) (sim) (a) =
    let n = sim.frames
    in let sa = (0.0 - state.v) / (2.0 * real-max n 1.0) * exp-real ((0.0 - n) / brake-decay)
    in if sa < a then sa else a

 Do not crawl below the corner's entry speed approaching it -- UNLESS he is
 gawking at the pigs, where staying at the slow gawk speed and taking the corner
 slow beats snapping back up, because he never re-accelerates.

  clamp-v : RiderState, Segment, Real, Real, Boolean -> Real
  clamp-v (state) (seg) (v-end) (v0) (near) =
    let v1 = if v0 > v-max then v-max else v0
    in let v2 = if v1 < 0.0 then 0.0 else v1
    in if near then (if v2 < v-end then (if gawk-engaged state seg then v2 else v-end) else v2) else v2

  get-forward-accel-decel : RiderState, Segment -> Real
  get-forward-accel-decel (state) (seg) =
    let a0 = if real-abs state.tilt >= tilt-hold then 0.0 else a-accel
    in let v-end = if seg.terminates then 0.0 else turn-speed seg.exit-angle
    in let near = (seg.length - state.along) <= approach-intersection-dist
    in let a1 = if near then corner-brake state seg v-end a0 else a0
    in let a3 = pig-gate state seg (cat-gate state seg a1)
    in let a4 = shoulder-brake state seg a3
    in clamp-v state seg v-end (state.v + a4) near - state.v

Section: Deciding

  Decision = record {
    tilt-step : Real,
    accel : Real
  }

 The acceleration is chosen for the LEANED state, not the current one: he commits
 to the lean first, and the throttle then answers the bike he is about to be.

  decide : RiderState, Segment -> Decision
  decide (state) (seg) =
    let tilt-step = best-tilt-correction state seg - state.tilt
    in Decision { tilt-step = tilt-step, accel = get-forward-accel-decel (with-tilt state (state.tilt + tilt-step)) seg }

Section: Crossing a Seam

 Re-express the rider onto the next segment as he commits to the turn: the same
 physical point, read in the next frame. Speed and lean carry; the frame-relative
 yaw rotates by the turn while the ABSOLUTE heading does not, because the crossing
 changes the frame and not the bearing.

 His eyes go back on the road across the seam, but the FOCUS carries, so its slow
 re-widen keeps easing out instead of snapping open.

  rider-state-for-next-segment : RiderState, List Segment -> RiderState
  rider-state-for-next-segment (rs) (segs) =
    let seg = list-at segs rs.segment
    in let hw = seg.width / 2.0
    in let theta = seg.exit-angle
    in let sgn = if seg.exit-right then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0
    in let c = r-cos theta
    in let s = r-sin theta
    in let da = rs.along - (seg.length + hw * s)
    in let dx = rs.across - sgn * hw * (1.0 - c)
    in RiderState { segment = seg.exit-to, along = c * da + sgn * s * dx, across = (0.0 - sgn) * s * da + c * dx, yaw = rs.yaw - sgn * theta, v = rs.v, tilt = rs.tilt, heading = rs.heading, gaze-yaw = 0.0, focus = rs.focus }

Section: One Frame

 Decide the controls, run them through the physics, then resolve the road graph BY
 POSITION: commit to the next segment the moment his real point is inside it.

 At the terminus there is no segment to lead out, so he is clamped to the end and
 stopped. The snap fires once he has crept to the line OR slowed to a crawl inside
 the braking zone -- because the kinematic brake would otherwise Zeno-stall him a
 hair short and leave him stopped forever, never finished.

  finish-clamp : RiderState, Segment -> RiderState
  finish-clamp (moved) (seg) =
    let in-zone = (seg.length - moved.along) < approach-intersection-dist
    in if moved.along >= seg.length then stopped-at moved seg.length
       else if in-zone then (if moved.v < 0.1 then stopped-at moved seg.length else moved)
       else moved

  stopped-at : RiderState, Real -> RiderState
  stopped-at (s) (at) =
    RiderState { segment = s.segment, along = at, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw, v = 0.0, tilt = s.tilt, heading = s.heading, gaze-yaw = s.gaze-yaw, focus = s.focus }

  resolve-cross : RiderState, Segment, List Segment -> RiderState
  resolve-cross (moved) (seg) (segs) =
    let on-next = rider-state-for-next-segment moved segs
    in if real-abs on-next.across < (list-at segs seg.exit-to).width / 2.0 then on-next else moved

 The gaze is its own step and runs AFTER the bike has moved, because it reads the
 RESOLVED segment and position.

  get-next-rider-state : RiderState, List Segment -> RiderState
  get-next-rider-state (state) (segs) =
    let seg = list-at segs state.segment
    in let dec = decide state seg
    in let moved = simulate-rider-step state dec.tilt-step dec.accel
    in let resolved = if seg.terminates then finish-clamp moved seg else resolve-cross moved seg segs
    in next-rider-gaze resolved segs

  is-finished : RiderState, List Segment -> Boolean
  is-finished (s) (segs) =
    let seg = list-at segs s.segment
    in if seg.terminates then s.along >= seg.length else False
